<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/macrobenchmark-and-perfetto.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">A pull request turns the startup benchmark red. Before changing code, check that it still loads the same content on the same device and compilation state. A faster empty screen is not a successful experiment.</p>

Macrobenchmark supplies repeated journey measurements; a trace helps explain a specific change.

<!-- @after Macrobenchmark vs Microbenchmark :: visual -->

### Choose evidence for the question

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" data-cols="3"><div class="node"><strong>Did the journey regress?</strong><small>Repeated Macrobenchmark measurement with a defined boundary.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Where did time go?</strong><small>The corresponding Perfetto interval and thread/resource tracks.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Who experiences it?</strong><small>Field measurements segmented by release, journey and device cohort.</small></div></div><figcaption>These complement one another. A trace from one iteration does not describe an entire production distribution.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Perfetto Trace Workflow :: animation -->

### From a red number to a testable cause

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">From a red number to a testable cause</span><div class="stage viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node" data-el="control" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Controls</strong><small>Build, device, data, compilation</small></div><div class="node" data-el="metric" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Metric</strong><small>Repeated journey result</small></div><div class="node" data-el="trace" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Trace</strong><small>The regressed interval</small></div><div class="node" data-el="hypothesis" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Hypothesis</strong><small>One evidence-supported change</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Control", "caption": "Confirm the same useful-content condition and test configuration. Record thermal/device state and avoid unstable external data.", "state": {"control": "active"}, "add": ["control", "metric", "trace", "hypothesis"]}, {"label": "Measure", "caption": "Use repeated measurements and retain raw results. Decide whether the observed difference exceeds the experiment’s noise.", "state": {"control": "done", "metric": "active"}}, {"label": "Explain", "caption": "Open a corresponding trace at the measured boundary. Inspect running, waiting and downstream work before assigning a cause.", "state": {"metric": "done", "trace": "active"}}, {"label": "Challenge", "caption": "Change one supported cause and repeat. If the boundary changed or content stopped loading, reject the apparent win.", "state": {"trace": "done", "hypothesis": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after CI and Regression Policy :: staff -->

Write down the benchmark question before running it: which journey, which metric, which device class and which compilation state can distinguish the suspected regression? Retain repetitions and raw traces, including the slow runs, rather than selecting a favourable sample. Use the trace to connect a metric change to a responsible interval. Only then set a gate whose noise tolerance matches observed variation; an unstable threshold can block useful changes and admit harmful ones.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Why keep raw traces with the regression?</summary>

They let a reviewer inspect the measured interval and test the proposed cause.

</details>

<details><summary>A new build is faster because the feed selector never found content. Accept it?</summary>

No. The experiment no longer measures the intended successful journey.

</details>

<details><summary>Can one trace prove p99 improved for users?</summary>

No. A trace explains one interval; distributions and representative cohorts require broader measurements.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Macrobenchmark setup](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/benchmarking/macrobenchmark-overview), [Metrics](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/benchmarking/macrobenchmark-metrics).
